\section{Introduction}

Reading comprehension (RC) is a critical task in natural language processing, with a primary goal of determining whether a given question can be answered using a provided text. While most existing RC research focuses on single-document scenarios, real-world applications often involve answering questions across multiple documents. For instance, answering a question about the 1968 Olympics might require synthesizing information from separate documents regarding the US team, the US sports system, and the history of track and field. We term this the \emph{Multi-Document Multi-Question} (MDMQ) setting. This approach is particularly advantageous for questions that demand information synthesis from various sources.

To our knowledge, MDMQ represents the first setting for both RC and question answering (QA). Consequently, we have created a large-scale MDMQ RC dataset, MDMQ-RC, which consists of multiple documents for each question. We also evaluate several existing models in this new setting and propose a novel MDMQ Reader (MDR). The MDR employs a shared encoder for both documents and questions, allowing it to leverage the semantic information of the documents to better understand the question. Furthermore, the MDR incorporates a novel attention mechanism that enables the model to focus on the most relevant documents for a specific question. Experimental results on the MDMQ-RC dataset show that our MDR significantly outperforms other models in the MDMQ setting.

