\title{PDGM: A Local Privacy-Preserving Mechanism for Deep Learning}
\label{PDGM}

\section*{1. Introduction}
Deep learning has become a cornerstone of modern artificial intelligence, yet it often demands massive datasets, raising significant privacy concerns. Traditional methods for protecting data in deep learning focus on centralized training, but federated learning offers an alternative by training models locally on user devices. However, this decentralization introduces new privacy risks, as model updates can leak sensitive local data. This paper presents a new method for data privacy protection in federated learning that leverages a local privacy-preserving mechanism based on deep generative models. Specifically, we use a deep generative model to learn the distribution of local data and then generate synthetic data to replace the original data during training. To our knowledge, this is the first application of deep generative models for data privacy protection in federated learning. Moreover, we propose a privacy-utility trade-off mechanism to balance privacy and utility. Our method is effective in protecting local data while achieving acceptable model performance. Additionally, our method is computationally efficient and easy to implement. The experiments show that our method outperforms existing methods in both data privacy protection and model performance. Furthermore, our method is more efficient than existing methods in terms of computational cost. The code for our method is available at https://github.com/chenyao92000/PDGM.

